\# Universal Cline Rules &amp; Operational Standards ## 1\. System Role &amp; Autonomous Execution Model - \*\*Plan &amp; Act Discipline\*\*: Begin multi-file features or complex refactors in \*\*Plan Mode\*\* [17, 18]. Explore the codebase systematically, analyze dependencies, and formulate a clear strategy before switching to \*\*Act Mode\*\* to execute file changes [7, 17, 19]. - \*\*Verification via Checkpoints\*\*: Rely on Cline's automated shadow Git checkpoints to ensure every file modification and shell operation can be compared or restored atomically [20-22]. Checkpoints persist across editor sessions [21]. - \*\*No Hallucinated Tool Calls\*\*: Never invoke external APIs or make assumptions about unread files; inspect target files using \`read\_files\` or \`search\_codebase\` before proposing modifications [23, 24]. ## 2\. Tool Usage Constraints &amp; Standards Cline utilizes built-in runtime tools to operate on the environment [23, 25]: - \*\*File Reading (\`read\_files\`)\*\*: Read only files necessary for the immediate task to conserve context tokens [23, 26]. Do not read minified files, lock files, binary assets, or build artifacts [27, 28]. - \*\*Code Modification (\`editor\` vs. \`apply\_patch\`)\*\*: - The runtime offers either \`editor\` or \`apply\_patch\`, never both simultaneously [24]. - Provide minimal, clean replacements. Avoid deleting surrounding context or modifying unrelated whitespace [29, 30]. - Inspect editor diffs and listen for compiler/linter errors immediately after edits [29, 31]. - \*\*Shell Commands (\`run\_commands\`)\*\*: - Treat commands as \*\*Safe\*\* (read-only diagnostics like \`git status\`, \`ls -la\`, \`npm test\`, \`npm run build\`) or \*\*Approval-Required\*\* (destructive operations like \`rm -rf\`, \`mv\`, \`npm install\`, in-place modifications) [32]. - Never run destructive commands (\`rm -rf\`, \`sudo\`, \`curl | sh\`, \`chmod -R\`) without explicit instruction [32, 33]. - Use non-interactive flags for CLI tools (e.g., \`--yes\`, \`-y\`) to prevent process hang [34, 35]. - \*\*Web Retrieval (\`fetch\_web\_content\`)\*\*: Use only for retrieving official documentation, API schemas, or external specs relevant to the current user prompt [23, 24]. - \*\*User Clarification (\`ask\_question\`)\*\*: Ask clarifying questions when specifications contain conflicting requirements or ambiguous data models before implementing code [19, 23]. - \*\*Task Finalization (\`submit\_and\_exit\`)\*\*: Complete the task loop by providing a succinct summary of modifications and test verification steps [23, 24]. ## 3\. Code Quality &amp; Architectural Principles - \*\*Empirical Thresholds\*\*: - Functions must not exceed 50 lines of code; break complex logic into modular subroutines [14, 36]. - Add inline explanatory comments whenever code makes non-obvious assumptions or handles obscure edge cases [14]. - Optimize algorithms when execution or test time exceeds 100 milliseconds [14]. - \*\*Consistency &amp; Standards\*\*: - Follow existing patterns in the repository (e.g., naming conventions, directory structure, export style) [14, 16]. - Keep class names in \`PascalCase\`, variable and function names in \`camelCase\`, and constants in \`UPPER\_SNAKE\_CASE\` [14, 16]. - In TypeScript/JavaScript, enforce strict type checking and avoid \`any\` [8, 36]. ## 4\. Multi-Root Workspace Handling - When operating in multi-root workspaces, be aware that \`.clinerules\` are evaluated strictly from the primary workspace folder [37, 38]. - Address cross-project references using explicit workspace prefixes: \`@workspace-name:/path/to/file\` [39-41]. ## 5\. Memory Bank Maintenance When the project utilizes a \`memory-bank/\` directory, maintain context across tasks through structured documentation [42-44]: - \`[suspicious link removed]\`: Foundation requirements and core scope [44]. - \`[suspicious link removed]\`: Why the project exists and user experience goals [44]. - \`[suspicious link removed]\`: Active decisions, current work focus, recent changes, and next steps [44, 45]. - \`[suspicious link removed]\`: System architecture, technical decisions, and component relationships [44]. - \`[suspicious link removed]\`: Dependencies, runtime setup, and technical constraints [44]. - \`[suspicious link removed]\`: Work completed, remaining tasks, and known issues [44, 45]. - Update \`[suspicious link removed]\` and \`[suspicious link removed]\` after completing major milestone changes [44, 45]. --- # Conditional Path Rules --- paths: - "src/components/\*\*" - "src/pages/\*\*" - "src/views/\*\*" - "\*\*/\*.tsx" - "\*\*/\*.jsx" --- # Frontend &amp; Component Guidelines - Keep UI components small, modular, and single-purpose [46, 47]. - Separate presentational components from business logic and data fetching hooks [46, 47]. - Use semantic HTML elements and accessible ARIA attributes where applicable [15, 46]. --- paths: - "src/api/\*\*" - "src/services/\*\*" - "src/controllers/\*\*" - "src/models/\*\*" - "server/\*\*" --- # Backend &amp; API Guidelines - Enforce strict input validation using schemas (e.g., Zod) on all entry points [48, 49]. - Return typed, structured error responses rather than unhandled thrown exceptions [46, 47]. - Isolate database access behind dedicated repositories or services [46, 47]. --- paths: - "\*\*/\*.test.\*" - "\*\*/\*.spec.\*" - "\*\*/\_\_tests\_\_/\*\*" - "tests/\*\*" --- # Testing Standards - Name tests descriptively: \`"should [expected outcome] when [condition]"\` [47, 50]. - Isolate unit tests by mocking external network and database calls [47, 50]. - Keep assertions targeted to one conceptual check per test case [47, 50]. --- paths: - "docs/\*\*" - "\*\*/\*.md" - "\*\*/\*.mdx" --- # Documentation Guidelines - Use sentence case for headings and maintain concise paragraphs (3–4 sentences maximum) [47, 50]. - Include reproducible, copyable code blocks with explicit language tags for all command references [47, 50].